From 538072854cc3e9672bd4f63c163423bf5a805d08 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 18:55:50 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] DOS-PORT: patch 0316 -- an opt-in per-tick log of the coarse
 timing scopes

WHY: the port's coarse scopes (update_game_once, render_to_screen,
scene_construct) report whole-run totals only, so no figure can leave a
tick out. A benchmark that must exclude the room-entry tick (dosags
LAB-MMD 47.2: the room-50 fade-in tick can inflate the character-select
logic mean by up to 0.92 ms/tick) or a click's pathfinding hitch (the
in-play cell, LAB-MMD 50.2) could only be bounded, never computed.

WHAT: DOSAGS_TICKLOG=<ticks>, read once at startup, allocates a record
buffer (4 records a tick plus 256). Every close of the three coarse
scopes then appends (loop counter, kind, us). The us is the same clamped
value the run totals add, so the log sums to the totals. "Now in room" and
each dequeued click or key append an event record. At exit the buffer
goes to TICKS.BIN ("TLOG", version 1, records, dropped, capacity, then
two 32-bit words a record: the loop counter, then kind << 28 | value),
and RUNMANI.LOG gets a "ticklog" line. Records are in time order. A
tick's render, scene, room and input records come before its
update_game_once close, because the loop counter advances between
render and that close.

COST when unset: the buffer pointer stays null, so each coarse-scope
close pays one byte test and one pointer test. There is no allocation,
no file and no clock read. The scope's kind rides in the padding after
its existing bool, so the scope object does not grow. Fine scopes
(kind 0) pay the byte test only when fine timing is on.

Removal: none planned. It is a measurement switch like 0315's, off
unless asked for.
